WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.890
Joe Avella: The Magic
Castle is an exclusive club

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:06.260
run by the Academy of Magical Arts,

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.670
but only their members
and guests are allowed in.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:13.253
Founded in 1963, The Magic
Castle looms over Hollywood.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.300
It's pretty hard to miss.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.680
I've been curious about The
Magic Castle since I moved here,

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:33.610
and, doing research, turns
out it's incredibly exclusive.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:35.460
There's really only two ways to get in:

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:36.700
be a member

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.033
or get an invite from a member.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.230
Joe Furlow: We are a private club,

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:43.060
very similar to a country
club or yacht club,

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.300
we just deal with the art of magic.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.190
Joe Avella: I got an
exclusive tour of The Castle,

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.570
which, in addition to the
many bars, magic theaters,

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:50.880
and full dining area,

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.710
it also features a séance room,

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:55.500
a library filled with decades
of secret magic tricks,

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.650
and even ghosts.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:00.125
This is one of most exclusive
clubs in Los Angeles,

00:01:00.125 --> 00:01:02.320
and they have been nice
enough to let us in early

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.900
to show us all the ins and outs
of this really great place.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.600
For starters, to get into this place,

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.610
you have to enter through
a trick bookshelf.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.970
Rmax Goodwin: So, you say
to the owl, "Open, sesame."

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.479
Joe: Mo, you wanna do the honors?

00:01:13.479 --> 00:01:14.921
Mo: Open, sesame.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.250
Rmax: Come on in, after you!

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:22.170
Joe: Cool!

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.160
Magician and mentalist Rmax Goodwin

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:25.980
was nice enough to give us a tour.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.550
Rmax: So, this is the main bar,

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:30.641
and at any given night from,

00:01:30.641 --> 00:01:32.400
let's say, five to midnight,

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.970
this place is packed with
magicians and their guests,

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.480
and people are here to
see incredible magic

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:39.660
in a bunch of different showrooms,

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:43.170
have dinner, have
drinks, have a good time.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.299
Joe: Oh, do you notice
everyone is dressed up?

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:48.092
The Magic Castle has an
incredibly strict dress code.

00:01:48.092 --> 00:01:49.792
I'm the kind of guy, if
you see me in a suit,

00:01:49.792 --> 00:01:52.307
I'm either on my way
to a funeral or court.

00:01:52.307 --> 00:01:55.100
But with The Magic Castle,
they have a strict dress code,

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.522
no exceptions.

00:01:56.522 --> 00:01:59.270
Rmax: So, now we're going to
enter the close-up gallery

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:00.130
of The Magic Castle,

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.340
which is a really unique theater,
it's an intimate setting,

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:03.640
seats only 30 people,

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:04.980
it's a really interactive space

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.930
where you can see magic really close-up.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.400
Joe: What type of magic is
best for a room like this?

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.570
Rmax: So, I would say close-up magic.

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:14.490
And close-up magic is defined
in the magic community

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:16.310
as magic that breaks the fourth wall.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.168
Joe: Can I put you on the spot right now

00:02:17.168 --> 00:02:18.001
and show us a trick?

00:02:18.001 --> 00:02:19.320
Rmax: Yeah, sure. Of course.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.153
Mo.
Mo: Yes.

00:02:20.153 --> 00:02:21.470
Rmax: Normally, if you see a magician,

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:22.910
they would say something
like, "Pick a card."

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:25.270
Right? That's the classic
thing that you're expecting.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:26.887
I just want you to name one.

00:02:26.887 --> 00:02:28.040
Any card you want.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:28.997
Joe: No way. I'm already like,

00:02:28.997 --> 00:02:30.341
"No way is this gonna happen."
Rmax: From a deck of cards,

00:02:30.341 --> 00:02:31.174
anything you'd like.

00:02:31.174 --> 00:02:32.375
Mo: Jack of spades.

00:02:32.375 --> 00:02:33.320
Rmax: Jack of spades, OK.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.797
And why don't you hold
out a hand, palm up?

00:02:35.797 --> 00:02:36.630
Perfect.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.650
So, we will use something like,

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:39.655
all right, we have a queen here.

00:02:39.655 --> 00:02:40.975
Mo: All right, that wasn't my card.

00:02:40.975 --> 00:02:42.341
Rmax: That's OK, we're getting there.

00:02:42.341 --> 00:02:43.950
Joe: Start over!

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:44.783
Rmax: Queen of diamonds.

00:02:44.783 --> 00:02:45.616
So, hold out your hand like this, Mo.

00:02:45.616 --> 00:02:46.890
Joe: You said jack of spades?
Mo: Yeah.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:47.723
Joe: OK, I already forgot.

00:02:47.723 --> 00:02:48.971
Rmax: Jack of spades,
all right. So, obviously,

00:02:48.971 --> 00:02:51.224
you could have said something
like a three, right?

00:02:51.224 --> 00:02:52.980
Watch this.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.390
That's it.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:55.740
It's done.
Joe: What's it?

00:02:57.425 --> 00:02:58.430
Get the....

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:00.087
What the h---?

00:03:00.087 --> 00:03:02.007
What the h---!

00:03:03.032 --> 00:03:04.586
Will we be able to see how you did that?

00:03:04.586 --> 00:03:05.419
Because we just filmed that.

00:03:05.419 --> 00:03:06.644
Like, if we got back,
will we see, like...?

00:03:06.644 --> 00:03:08.460
Mo: You cannot do that!
Rmax: You can't see anything.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:09.304
Joe: What? Cannot do what?

00:03:09.304 --> 00:03:11.180
Mo: You can't tell magician secrets.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.249
Joe: No, I'm just saying, we filmed it.

00:03:13.249 --> 00:03:14.325
So, like, if I'm watching the video...

00:03:14.325 --> 00:03:15.501
Mo: Oh, like in slow motion?

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.638
Rmax: Do you wanna name one?

00:03:22.638 --> 00:03:24.100
Any card you'd like, 52 cards.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.130
Mo: Do you remember the cards?

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:26.291
Joe: Yeah, I do remember the cards.

00:03:26.291 --> 00:03:28.400
Rmax: Don't come up with, like,
queen of jacks or something.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.820
Joe: OK, OK.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.978
I'll go with the

00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:34.170
eight of hearts.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:35.003
Rmax: Eight of hearts.

00:03:35.003 --> 00:03:37.040
And did you feel like
that was a free choice,

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:38.710
or did I kind of make you say that?

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:40.064
Joe: You didn't make me say
it, but I'm working hard

00:03:40.064 --> 00:03:42.071
to pick a card that I don't
think you think I'm thinking.

00:03:42.071 --> 00:03:43.100
Rmax: Oh, OK.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.340
So maybe there was some
reverse psychology, though.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:46.690
Joe: I said eight of hearts?
Rmax: Eight of hearts.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:47.523
Joe: Yeah.

00:03:47.523 --> 00:03:48.508
Rmax: So, the reason I'm asking

00:03:48.508 --> 00:03:49.916
is that the eight of
hearts is the one card

00:03:49.916 --> 00:03:51.441
I always leave in the box.

00:03:51.441 --> 00:03:53.089
Mo: F---!

00:03:53.089 --> 00:03:53.922
Sorry!

00:03:53.922 --> 00:03:54.755
Joe: What the...?

00:03:54.755 --> 00:03:56.175
Rmax: I always leave a card in the box.

00:03:56.175 --> 00:03:58.008
Joe: Dude, what the h---?

00:04:00.043 --> 00:04:01.025
I'm, like....

00:04:01.025 --> 00:04:03.170
Rmax: Don't worry, it wears
off in, like, a week or so.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:05.068
Joe: I don't feel comfortable
being with this guy anymore.

00:04:05.068 --> 00:04:06.561
This is weird.

00:04:06.561 --> 00:04:09.114
Like, later he's just gonna be like,

00:04:09.114 --> 00:04:12.100
my watch is gonna appear
on his wrist or something.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.338
I'm keeping my hand on my wallet
for the rest of this thing.

00:04:14.338 --> 00:04:16.772
Next, we went to one of my
favorite rooms in The Castle:

00:04:16.772 --> 00:04:19.360
the Harry Houdini Séance Chamber.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.240
Joe Furlow: Houdini was the best.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:24.210
He really defined what magic is today,

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.285
and this was back in the '20s.

00:04:26.285 --> 00:04:30.360
He passed away on October 31, 1926,

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.523
and he's still known as the
greatest magician of all.

00:04:34.475 --> 00:04:36.280
Back there, we've got one of the trunks

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:37.910
that he would escape out of.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.180
Joe Avella: Houdini was
obsessed with séances,

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.210
attempting to reach his
mother in the afterlife.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.718
Joe Furlow: We do the
Houdini séance experience

00:04:44.718 --> 00:04:46.101
inside that room.

00:04:46.101 --> 00:04:48.660
It is our only private
dining space that we have

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:50.086
here at The Castle.

00:04:50.086 --> 00:04:53.890
It is a very sacred room, a
coveted room, so to speak.

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:54.870
Rmax: When Houdini was famous,

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.360
there was a couple other magicians

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:57.360
who were dueling at the time,

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:58.870
one of whom was Dante.

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:01.510
And Houdini and Dante had a big rivalry,

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.070
almost like a Hollywood rivalry,

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:04.940
where they absolutely hated each other.

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:07.500
And so we have another
room here at The Castle,

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:08.800
part of the dining area,

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.070
that's called the Dante Room, right?

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:12.800
So, when there was a fire here,

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.025
maybe six or seven years ago,

00:05:16.025 --> 00:05:17.900
parts of The Castle were
absolutely destroyed,

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.490
or not destroyed, but were damaged;

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:22.730
that was the Dante Room.

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:24.810
This room, though, the
Houdini Séance Room,

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:26.160
was completely spared.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:27.860
And what's really interesting

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:30.840
is that Houdini died on Halloween, right?

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.060
And the fire happened on Halloween.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.690
So, some people speculate
that maybe the ghosts,

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:40.140
or, I don't know, somebody
involved in Houdini's past,

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:42.050
may have spared Houdini's room,

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.237
and just to spite Dante, said,

00:05:44.237 --> 00:05:46.750
"Yeah, let's burn that little area down."

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:47.745
Joe Avella: And, quick epilogue,

00:05:47.745 --> 00:05:49.610
after this, we went to the Dante Room,

00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:51.321
and I had a little fun at his expense.

00:05:51.321 --> 00:05:53.255
Look at his jerk.
Rmax: There he is!

00:05:53.255 --> 00:05:55.810
Joe: Look at that smile.
Rmax: There he is.

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:57.826
Joe: He makes me want to
set this room on fire.

00:05:57.826 --> 00:05:59.930
Anyway, later that night we
had dinner in the Dante Room,

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:00.763
and it was great.

00:06:00.763 --> 00:06:01.860
I got a little bit carried away,

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:02.990
and when the bill came,

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:05.507
it was like 250 bucks.

00:06:05.507 --> 00:06:06.840
It was like Dante was staring down at me,

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.647
as if to say, "Who looks
like the jerk now?"

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:13.840
This video's a little too anti-Dante,

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:15.726
and I feel bad about it, all right?

00:06:15.726 --> 00:06:17.317
I'm sure he's a good dude.
Rmax: Yeah, who knows?

00:06:17.317 --> 00:06:18.480
Maybe he'll end up doing something to you.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:19.990
Joe: Yeah. Oh, God!

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:21.400
The Castle has several bars.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.830
My favorite is the Owl Bar,

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:25.670
named so for the fortune-telling
owl perched above.

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:27.176
Rmax: Archimedes will answer questions

00:06:27.176 --> 00:06:30.426
of both members and guests at any time.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:33.600
Joe: Guests of The Magic Castle

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:35.610
have several magic shows to choose from,

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.370
and they go pretty much all night.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:40.061
Small rooms, big rooms, magic at the bar.

00:06:40.061 --> 00:06:43.278
You're pretty much surrounded
by amazing magic all night.

00:06:43.278 --> 00:06:46.331
The most secretive part
of The Magic Castle

00:06:46.331 --> 00:06:48.265
is their library of magic.

00:06:49.881 --> 00:06:52.315
Which, of course, I was not let in.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:54.833
Sorry.

00:06:54.833 --> 00:06:57.570
Joe Furlow: We have books in
our library dating back to 1786

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:59.580
that show exactly how magic is done.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:01.840
That library is probably
the best magic library

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:02.840
in the world.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:04.110
The library is the one area

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:07.710
that we'd like to continue
to think of as sacred ground.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.601
Rmax: And this is the library.

00:07:09.601 --> 00:07:10.520
Joe Avella: Do you want
to check the handle,

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:11.419
see if it's unlocked?

00:07:11.419 --> 00:07:12.686
Rmax: Let's give it a shot.

00:07:12.686 --> 00:07:13.985
Mo: Can you work magic on it?

00:07:15.944 --> 00:07:16.777
Rmax: No.

00:07:16.777 --> 00:07:17.780
Joe Furlow: I think, when you come here

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:21.650
and you are amazed by our
professionals that we have in here

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:22.670
on a week-to-week basis,

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:26.050
it takes you back to that
first magic trick that you saw,

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:27.577
at whatever age it was.

00:07:27.577 --> 00:07:29.530
It was that sense of bewilderment.

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:32.350
And I think in today's
world, and today's society,

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:34.050
we need a little bit more of that.

